      "math_academy_id": "q-174983",
      "sequence_position": 7,
      "problem": "Let $I=[0,1]⊂R$ and ${X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{2}].$ Which of the following represents ${⋂}_{i\\in I}{X}_{i}?$",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{⋂}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}={1}\\times [0,{1}^{2}]={(1,k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(1,0)$ to the point $(1,1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/6b6a47e464623fe9c572a52f7c71377136e68f41dcfd641920634b415568d7f5.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,{i}^{2}]={(i,{k}^{2})\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,{i}^{2}).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14124149/assets/c67cd0b545f0fc779d2977e38fab8164eb3b0161a18e32a615c71d88d1c30b68.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $∅,$ the empty set.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
